A(('p', 'The table records Jesus’ words. It adds nothing to them.'))
A(('call', 'The Stone Passage and the Psalm',
   'The Psalm reads: “The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner” (Psalm 118:22). Jesus’ words as written in the three accounts read “rejected” where the Psalm reads “refused,” and “the head of the corner” where the Psalm reads “the head stone of the corner.” Jesus then adds, in Matthew and Luke, words about falling on the stone that the Psalm does not contain. This section records the words as they are written in each place and does not decide how they relate.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What Matthew reports. Matthew does not say that Jesus named the husbandmen. He says: “And when the chief priests and Pharisees had heard his parables, they perceived that he spake of them” (21:45). That is Matthew’s report of what the chief priests and Pharisees perceived. It is not a sentence in which Jesus identifies the husbandmen. Mark says “they knew that he had spoken the parable against them” (12:12), and Luke says “they perceived that he had spoken this parable against them” (20:19). The three accounts say what the hearers perceived; none of them records Jesus saying, “You are the husbandmen.”'))
A(('p', '4.4 What Jesus does not identify. Jesus does not identify the householder or the lord of the vineyard. Jesus does not identify the husbandmen. Jesus does not identify the servants, the first or the later. Jesus does not identify the son. Jesus does not identify the vineyard. Jesus does not identify the far country. Jesus does not identify the fruits. Jesus does not identify the nation of Matthew 21:43. Jesus does not state in these verses who the builders are or who the stone is.'))
