A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification of the parts, none is given here. A reader who says what the vineyard is, who the son is, who the servants are or which people are the nation of 21:43 supplies what the Lord did not. The words “a nation bringing forth the fruits thereof” are reported exactly as they stand, and no group is named for them.'))
A(('p', '4.5 The accounts do not borrow from each other. Matthew’s statement about the kingdom (21:43) is Matthew’s alone. Mark’s “wellbeloved” (12:6) and Luke’s “God forbid” (20:16) belong to those accounts. None of them is carried into another account, and none is carried into a different parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation of the story is given, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It assigns no identity to any part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Each account tells of an owner who plants a vineyard, lets it out to tenants and goes away. When the season comes he sends for the fruit, and the tenants beat, wound or kill those he sends. He sends a son, saying they will reverence him, and they kill the son to take the inheritance. The meaning of the comparison is in what the story shows about the owner, the tenants and the consequence.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The vineyard is entrusted.** The owner plants, prepares and lets it out; what the tenants hold, they hold from him.',
    '**Fruit is owed.** The owner sends “that they might receive the fruits of it” (Matthew 21:34), and the story treats the refusal to render it as the tenants’ fault.',
    '**The owner is patient.** He sends again and again, and in Matthew “more than the first” (21:36). The sending does not stop at the first refusal.',
    '**The refusal grows.** The servants are beaten, killed and stoned; the son is cast out and killed. The story moves from refusing the fruit to taking the inheritance.',
    '**The consequence is stated.** The owner’s coming is asked about, and the answer is destruction of the tenants and the vineyard given to others (Matthew 21:41; Mark 12:9; Luke 20:16).',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers had asked by what authority Jesus acted. The parable is told in answer to that setting, and the accounts say that the chief priests, the scribes and the Pharisees perceived that it was told against them (Matthew 21:45; Mark 12:12; Luke 20:19). They were meant to understand that those entrusted with a vineyard are accountable to its owner, that the refusal of his messengers and of his son is a grave thing, and that the owner’s coming is followed by a consequence.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the consequence. The force is the force of a limit. The owner’s patience is shown in the sending, and the consequence is shown in the question about his coming. Matthew adds a statement about the kingdom (21:43) and about the stone (21:44), which the other accounts do not record. Those words are reported as Jesus’ own in Section 4 and are left without an identification of the nation.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. What is entrusted must yield its fruit. Messengers are not to be refused. The refusal of the son is the last and gravest refusal. A consequence follows the owner’s coming. These statements arise from the comparison and do not say that the vineyard, the tenants, the servants or the son represent anything.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say who the tenants, the servants or the son are, and it does not say what the vineyard is. It does not say which nation Matthew 21:43 means. It does not turn any action in the story into a step by which a person is saved. It does not draw a meaning from another parable into this one.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The wicked tenants are told with a vineyard, and other parables in Matthew and Luke also use vineyards, feasts, fields and servants. They are kept apart from their neighbors here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 21:28', '“Son, go work to day in my vineyard.”', 'The Two Sons. It comes just before the wicked tenants in Matthew, in the same temple setting. Its vineyard and its ending are its own and are not carried into the tenants.'],
    ['Matt 22:3', '“And sent forth his servants to call them that were bidden to the wedding”', 'The Wedding Banquet. It follows in Matthew, with servants sent and refused. Its king, its son’s marriage and its burned city are its own.'],
    ['Luke 14:17', '“And sent his servant at supper time to say to them that were bidden”', 'The Great Banquet. A different setting, a supper at a house, and a servant sent to the invited. Its excuses and its filling of the house are not found in the vineyard story.'],
    ['Matt 13:24', '“a man which sowed good seed in his field”', 'The Wheat and the Weeds. A field, an enemy and a harvest. Jesus’ explanation of the field (13:38) belongs to that parable only and is not applied to the vineyard.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The wicked tenants are told in the temple after a question about authority, in the last days before the Cross. The Great Banquet is told at a meal (Luke 14:1, 15). The Wheat and the Weeds is told on the day of the parables by the sea (Matthew 13:1). A shared word such as “vineyard,” “servants” or “field” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The vineyard story has an owner, tenants, servants and a son. The Two Sons has a man and two sons. The Wedding Banquet has a king, servants and guests. The Great Banquet has a man, a servant and the invited. No character is carried from one parable to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The vineyard story moves from planting to refusal, to the killing of the son, to a question about the owner’s coming. The Two Sons moves from a command to a deed. The banquets move from an invitation to refusal and a filled hall. The Wheat and the Weeds moves from sowing to a delayed harvest. None of those movements is read into the vineyard story.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the vineyard of the Two Sons (Matthew 21:28) is not stated to be the vineyard of 21:33. Second, the servants who are killed in the Wedding Banquet (22:6) are not the servants of the vineyard. Third, the field of Matthew 13:38 is not the vineyard of Matthew 21:33, and the explanation Jesus gives there is not an explanation of this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17) and sent the Twelve “to the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:6). The wicked tenants belong to that ministry. The accounts place them in the temple at Jerusalem, in answer to chief priests, scribes and elders who had asked Him by what authority He acted.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The parable is not that Mystery, and “the kingdom of God” of Matthew 21:43 is a phrase of Jesus’ earthly ministry, spoken to those who stood before Him in the temple.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The vineyard, the tenants, the servants, the son and the nation of 21:43 are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and the story is not the way the Body is saved. The taking of the kingdom and the giving of it to a nation are reported as Jesus’ words in Matthew and are not read as a change in how anyone is saved or as a handing of Kingdom identity to the Body. The tenants’ refusal and the owner’s sending are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from the story. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of stewardship.** What is entrusted is held from another, and an account is owed for its fruit.',
    '**A lesson of hearing.** The owner’s messengers are sent more than once. A reader learns to hear what is sent and not to refuse it because it comes again.',
    '**A lesson of seriousness.** The story treats the refusal of the son as the gravest refusal. A reader learns to weigh what is said by the one sent last.',
    '**A lesson of humility.** The tenants take as theirs what was given to them in trust. A reader learns to hold what he has as received.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The consequence the story names is not turned into a warning laid on the Body, and the giving of the vineyard to others is not turned into the Body’s calling. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus tells of a man who plants a vineyard, lets it out to husbandmen and goes into a far country. At the season he sends servants for the fruit, and the husbandmen beat, wound and kill them. He sends his son, and they cast him out and kill him to take the inheritance. When the owner of the vineyard comes, Matthew records that the hearers say he will destroy those wicked men and let out the vineyard to others, Mark records Jesus saying it, and Luke records it with “God forbid” from those who heard. Jesus quotes the stone the builders rejected, and in Matthew He says that the kingdom of God shall be taken from those He addresses and given to a nation bringing forth the fruits thereof. Jesus explains none of the parts of the story and names no nation. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says what the vineyard, the tenants, the son or the nation is, or that makes any act in the story a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a vineyard is planted and let out; servants are sent and refused; the son is sent and killed; the owner’s coming is asked about and answered.',
    '**The meaning:** what is entrusted is owed its fruit; messengers are not to be refused; the refusal of the son is the gravest; a consequence follows the owner’s coming.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation of the parts; no identity for the vineyard, the tenants, the servants, the son or the nation; the three accounts are not merged.',
    '**The present lesson:** stewardship, hearing, seriousness and humility, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9
A(('h1', S9))
A(('bul', [
    '**Between accounts.** Matthew has a householder, servants sent in groups, the hearers’ answer (21:41), the kingdom statement (21:43) and the words on falling (21:44). Mark has one servant at a time, “wellbeloved,” and Jesus’ own answer (12:9). Luke has three servants, a long time, the owner’s purpose, “God forbid” and the words on falling (20:18). No detail is carried from one account to another.',
    '**Between speakers.** In Matthew the hearers answer the question. In Mark Jesus asks and answers it. In Luke no speaker is named for the answer.',
    '**Between Jesus’ words and the report of the hearers.** Jesus’ words are the stone passage, the kingdom statement and the words on falling. That the hearers perceived the story was told of them is the report of Matthew, Mark and Luke.',
    '**Between this parable and its neighbors.** The Two Sons, the Wedding Banquet, the Great Banquet and the Wheat and the Weeds have their own settings and details. The field of Matthew 13:38 is not the vineyard of Matthew 21:33.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the householder, the husbandmen, the servants, the son, the vineyard, the fruits or the nation of 21:43.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from another parable, from Isaiah 5 or from the epistles.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Sending, refusing, killing and taking are actions in a story. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
]))

